So let's first find the things being asked and work our way forward. First, we're being asked about how many values of a satisfy a given condition. We usually draw a conclusion in these questions by listing all the things requiring those conditions to be met: work backward until we reach our conclusions.</thinking>

Rewriting we have
\begin{align*} 
\Delta & = (2a+1)^2-4a^2 = 4a + 1
\end{align*}
We want that value of $a$ such that $4a+1=k^2$ and $k$ is an integer.
Rewriting in terms of $a$ gives
\begin{align*}
4a + 1 &= k^2\\
4a &= k^2 - 1\\
4a &= (k+1)(k-1)
\end{align*}
We can see that $a$, $k+1$ and $k-1$ are consecutive integers, with $a$ in the middle. The only several consecutive integers and the average of them is a positive integer are $1$ and $2$, we want $a=1$. We can check this works, because
\begin{align*}
4a &= 4\cdot 1\\
&=3\cdot 1
